# How far ahead can you see?
teaches: ["calculation/visualisation","safety/three-questions"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "Why look at checks, captures and threats first? How do you calculate three moves deep without moving the pieces?"
next: {"title":"Why do the opening rules exist?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/05-why-do-the-opening-rules-exist.cast"}

## Warm-up: count, then threaten
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 3r2k1/ppp1bppp/4p3/3r4/8/2N1P3/PPP1BPPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxd5, exd5]}
      - {fen: r2q1rk1/pb3ppp/1pp1p3/8/1n6/1P2P3/PBPNQPPP/R4RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qg4, g6, Qxb4]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: How far ahead can you see? Today, how to see three moves deep. First, two warm-ups from this week.
    draw position

B: Black has doubled rooks on the d-file. The rook on d5: two attackers, two defenders. I leave it alone.
    highlight piece_d5

A: Count what the pieces cost, not just how many. Which of your attackers is cheapest?
    highlight piece_c3 piece_d1

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Count what each piece costs.","Your knight."] themes ["counting"] voice a teaches ["calculation/counting"]
        right: Knight takes the rook, and the pawn takes back: a rook for a knight, and Black's extra exchange is gone. The numbers were equal; the values weren't.
        wrong: Two against two, but what does each of those pieces cost?

B: A rook for a knight, and the material's level again. I'd have counted two against two and walked away.
    highlight piece_d5

A: Second one. Black's knight on b4 has no guard.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: I'd chase it with pawn to a3.
    highlight piece_a2

A: Pawn to a3? The knight just jumps into c2 and forks your rook and pawn.
    point at.ref sq_c2 gesture circle

A: Find a move that makes two threats at once.
    highlight piece_e2 piece_b2

    pause 0.3

    ask "Make two threats." widget chess line true hints ["Your queen and bishop both aim at g7.","Which queen move also hits b4?"] themes ["threat"] voice a teaches ["safety/threats"]
        right: Queen to g4 threatens mate on g7 and the loose knight on b4. Black stops the mate, and the knight falls.
        wrong: Look for a queen move that threatens mate and attacks the knight.

B: Mate on one side, the knight on the other. Black can't stop both.
    point at.ref sq_g7 gesture circle

A: Both times, you saw to the end of the line. Today: how far you can see, and how to see further.
    highlight piece_b4

## Forcing moves first
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r2qkb1r/ppp1nppp/2np4/4p3/2B1P1b1/2NP1N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQ1RK1 w kq - 0 1, moves: [Bxf7+, Kxf7, Ng5+, Kg8, Qxg4], arrows: [{from: d1, to: g4}]}
      - {fen: 4q1k1/pp1brp2/2p3pp/3p4/3P4/1BP5/PP1Q1PPP/2R3K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qxh6]}
      - {fen: 4q1k1/pp1brp2/2p3pQ/3p4/3P4/1BP5/PP3PPP/2R3K1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Re1+, Rxe1, Qxe1#], flip: true, arrows: []}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: degroot
      title: Thought and Choice in Chess
      authors: Adriaan de Groot
      year: 1965
      finding: In think-aloud experiments, grandmasters did not search more moves or deeper than weaker players; they were better at choosing which moves to consider.
      url: https://www.chessprogramming.org/Adriaan_de_Groot
    - id: connors2011
      title: 'Expertise in complex decision making: the role of search in chess 70 years after de Groot'
      authors: Connors, Burns & Campitelli
      year: 2011
      finding: A modern think-aloud replication with grandmasters, masters, experts and class players supports de Groot's overall conclusions.
      url: https://ro.ecu.edu.au/ecuworks2011/610

    card title "How far ahead?"

A: Now that you can count a trade to the end: how far ahead can you see?
    draw position

B: Not far. Grandmasters see twenty moves ahead. I see one.
    highlight piece_c4

A: Nobody sees everything. The Dutch psychologist Adriaan de Groot found that grandmasters don't look at many more moves than club players. They look at better ones.
    highlight piece_d1 piece_c4 piece_f3

A: Here's an ordinary opening position. White to move.
    highlight piece_e8

B: I'd play h3, and ask the bishop where it's going.
    highlight piece_h2 piece_g4

A: After h3, Black has thirty-four replies. Nobody can calculate thirty-four.
    text many "34" x 905 y 500 font_size 80
    text many_l "replies" x 905 y 415 font_size 32
    parallel true

A: Knight takes e5 just loses the knight: the knight on c6 takes back.
    highlight piece_e5

A: But bishop takes f7 is check.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Black has two replies: take it, or walk the king to d7.
    text few "2" x 905 y 330 font_size 80
    text few_l "replies" x 905 y 245 font_size 32
    parallel true

A: That's why checks, captures and threats come first. They cut your opponent's answers down, so you can see to the end.
    highlight few

    erase many many_l few few_l

B: The king takes it, as most would. I've given a bishop for a pawn.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: That's where most players stop: one move deep. It's your move again. Checks, captures, threats.
    highlight piece_f7

    pause 0.3

    ask "Keep going." widget chess line true hints ["A check with the knight.","What does your queen see once the knight has moved?"] themes ["discovered attack","combination"] voice a teaches ["tactics/discovered-attack","calculation/combination"] pool false
        right: Knight to g5, check. The king steps away, and the queen takes the bishop on g4.
        wrong: Checks first: which knight move gives check, and what does it uncover?

B: The knight jumped with check, and suddenly the queen hit g4.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.2

A: That's the hard part of seeing it in your head. After each move, ask what changed.
    draw arrow_0

A: The knight left f3, and the queen's diagonal opened.
    point at.ref sq_f3 gesture circle

    erase arrow_0

A: Then count at the end: a bishop given, a bishop and a pawn won.
    animate plies_shown 5 duration 2.2

A: And Black's king can no longer castle.
    highlight piece_g8

A: That was your side of the board. But calculating is for both sides.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Queen takes h6. A free pawn, right next to his king.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Now sit in Black's chair: the board turns round.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Punish it." widget chess line true hints ["Checks first.","Your rook and queen stand on the e-file."] themes ["back rank"] voice a teaches ["safety/back-rank"]
        right: Rook to e1, check. The rook takes it, the queen takes back: mate on the back rank.
        wrong: Look at Black's checks first: who still guards e1?

B: Mate. My free pawn cost the game.
    point at.ref piece_g1 gesture circle

A: The queen and the rook both guarded e1.
    blocking false

    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.5

    highlight piece_d2 piece_c1

A: When the queen left, the rook was alone.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: So a line isn't over when you've taken something.
    highlight piece_h6

A: It's over when it's quiet: no checks, captures or threats for either side. Then you count.
    point at.ref sq_e1 gesture circle

## Your turn: see it to the end
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r2q1rk1/pb2bppp/1pn1p3/2p5/8/3B1N2/PPP1QPPP/R1BR2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxh7+, Kxh7, Rxd8]}
      - {fen: rnbq1rk1/pp1pbppp/2p2n2/4N3/8/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R b KQ - 0 1, moves: [Qa5+, Nc3, Qxe5+], flip: true}
      - {fen: 3r2k1/pp1r1ppp/8/2q5/3R4/8/PP1R1PPP/3Q2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rxd7, Rxd7, Rxd7]}
      - {fen: 5rk1/p4ppp/q4P2/8/1Q6/8/5PPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qxf8+, Kxf8, Rd8#]}
      - {fen: r5k1/pp3ppp/3b4/8/7q/1Q6/PP2BPPP/4R1K1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Qxh2+, Kf1, Qh1#], flip: true}
      - {fen: 3r2k1/pp1P1ppp/8/8/8/8/P4PPP/2R3K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rc8, Kf8, Rxd8+]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now you know the method: forcing moves first, each to a quiet end. Your turn: three puzzles, then a short test.
    draw position

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Checks first: your bishop.","What does the rook on d1 see once the bishop has gone?"] themes ["discovered attack"] voice a teaches ["tactics/discovered-attack"]
        right: Bishop takes h7, check. The king takes, and the rook takes the queen: a queen for a rook and a bishop.
        wrong: Which check moves a piece out of your rook's way?

A: Now you're Black, so the board turns round. An old friend: a loose piece.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Which white piece has no guard?","A queen check."] themes ["hanging piece"] voice a teaches ["safety/hanging-pieces"]
        right: Queen to a5, check. White blocks, and the queen takes the loose knight on e5.
        wrong: Find the white piece nobody guards, then a check that also hits it.

A: Now count. At first glance, Black's rook on d7 is attacked once and defended once.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: So it's safe.
    highlight piece_d7

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Count the pieces behind, too.","Your second rook and queen are on the d-file."] themes ["counting"] voice a teaches ["calculation/counting"]
        right: Rook takes, rook takes back, rook takes again. Your second rook and queen stand behind the first: three attackers against one defender, and you win a rook.
        wrong: Count every piece lined up on the d-file, front and back.

A: Not safe at all. A count includes the pieces lined up behind: here, the obvious capture was right.
    highlight piece_d1

A: Now the test: three positions, each two or three moves deep.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: See it to the end before your first move.
    highlight piece_g8

    ask "Mate in two." widget chess line true themes ["back rank"] voice a test day4 teaches ["safety/back-rank"]
        right: Queen takes f8, check. The king must take, and the rook mates on d8: the pawn on f6 covers e7, and his own pawns block f7 and g7.

A: Two. You're Black, so the board turns round.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    ask "Mate in two." widget chess line true themes ["mating pattern"] voice a test day4 teaches ["tactics/mating-patterns"]
        right: Queen takes h2, check, with the bishop behind her. The king runs to f1, and the queen mates on h1: his own rook, bishop and pawns block him in.

A: Last one. Black has two answers. See both.
    animate position_shown 5 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true themes ["combination"] voice a test day4 teaches ["calculation/combination"]
        right: Rook to c8. If the rook takes, the pawn takes back and queens with mate. So the king steps aside to f8, and your rook takes d8 with check: a whole rook up.

    erase position

    text score_big "Day 4 score" x 500 y 400 font_size 110

A: You scored {day4.score} of {day4.total}.
    results test day4

    pause 1

    erase score_big

A: So seeing further isn't a gift. It's choosing which moves to look at: forcing ones first,
    text syn_1 "forcing first" x 500 y 520 font_size 64

A: each to a quiet end,
    text syn_2 "to a quiet end" x 500 y 420 font_size 64

A: the board updated in your head.
    text syn_3 "update the board" x 500 y 320 font_size 64

    pause 0.5

    erase syn_1 syn_2 syn_3

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower.
    text hw_game "one slow game: 15 + 10" x 500 y 440 font_size 52

A: Then find its first mistake.
    text hw_mistake "find mistake 1" x 500 y 350 font_size 52

## Where next
end_page: true

    text end_next_kicker "Next" x 500 y 540 font_size 26 style.opacity 0.6
    link end_next href lecture:5 title "Why do the opening rules exist?" form title size 600 x 500 y 420

    link end_again href lecture:4 title "Watch again" form text mark replay open here x 500 y 250 font_size 28
    text end_count "4 of 30" x 500 y 180 font_size 22 style.opacity 0.6
